+private let obeliskReleasesFixture = #"""
+[{"tag_name": "v0.2.5", "prerelease": false, "draft": false, "published_at": "2026-10-08T19:06:20Z", "body": "Obelisk 0.2.5 brings together the work since 0.2.4: complete browsing of large session archives, reliable Copilot discovery across VS Code Stable and Insiders, local Kiro CLI history, and clearer setup documentation.\n\n## Highlights\n\n- **All your indexed sessions within reach.** The desktop catalogue no longer stops at the newest 1,000 sessions. Counts, metadata search, project/source filters, and oldest-first sorting cover the complete indexed history.\n- **Copilot history from both VS Code editions.** Discover Stable and Insiders by default, choose either folder independently in Settings, or select a custom folder.\n\n## Updates\n\n### Complete session browsing\n\n- Load the complete session metadata catalogue instead of only the newest 1,000 entries. Older indexed conversations remain reachable through the Sessions list and metadata search.\n- Make the Sessions badge, project navigation, and source/project filters cover the full catalogue, including projects whose conversations all fall outside the previously loaded subset.\n\n### Copilot history from Stable and Insiders\n\n- Fix the desktop worker handoff so default discovery indexes both **VS Code Stable and VS Code Insiders**, matching the folders watched for changes. Insiders-only installations are recognized as available sources.\n- Add two default-enabled folder switches in **Settings**, plus a custom-folder override. An explicitly selected custom folder continues to select that folder alone.\n\n### Kiro CLI history\n\n- Add **Kiro CLI** as a local history source, reading the native SQLite store and supported CLI/workspace JSON and JSONL session layouts. Discover standard local paths and support custom/exported roots.\n- Index prompts, assistant text and reasoning, tools/results, and reported V1/V2 token counts. Source SQLite databases are opened read-only.\n\n### Documentation and contribution guidance\n\n- Reorder the README around installation, everyday use, and deeper reference material, and add **README.zh-CN.md**.\n- Clarify where Copilot history is stored and how to configure its sources; document the new Kiro source in the READMEs, CLI documentation, and packaged skills.\n\n## Installation\n\n**macOS:** choose the DMG matching your Mac — **arm64** for Apple Silicon or **x64** for Intel — and install Obelisk.app in Applications. Both architectures are Developer ID signed and notarized.\n\n**Linux Intel/AMD 64-bit:** download `Obelisk-0.2.5-linux-amd64.deb` and install it with:\n\n```sh\nsudo apt install ./Obelisk-0.2.5-linux-amd64.deb\n```\n\nExisting **0.2.4 installations can upgrade through the app**. Use **Settings → About** to check manually, or use the sidebar update notice when the download is ready. Linux installation asks for administrator authorization. Existing **0.2.2 installations**, and older Linux builds without the updater, require one manual upgrade.\n\nThis release supplies **macOS DMG/ZIP and Linux amd64 Debian packages**. The Linux installer and two-version upgrades are verified on Ubuntu 22.04 and 24.04. Linux arm64, AppImage and Windows distribution/update channels remain outside this release. Shared Core and CLI documentation work is included in the source; the CLI keeps its separate npm release lifecycle.\n\n## Included work\n\nAll **6 merged pull requests** since [v0.2.4](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/releases/tag/v0.2.4), including documentation and release preparation.\n\n- **@tommy0103 (owner)** — Reorganize the README and add a Chinese version; apply Copilot settings/reconciliation fixes; and prepare the desktop release. Included PRs: [#224](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/224), [#225](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/225), [#226](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/226).\n- Clarify Copilot history sources and setup — @22Goose ([#215](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/215)).\n\n**Full changes:** [v0.2.4 → v0.2.5 release source](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/compare/v0.2.4...7e8ea3336b31c1660ebbc688924b1d12283494a4).\n"},
+ {"tag_name": "v0.2.4", "prerelease": false, "draft": false, "published_at": "2026-10-02T12:13:27Z", "body": "Obelisk 0.2.4 brings together the work since 0.2.2: more agent histories in one place, faster indexing and retrieval for large archives, more faithful transcripts, signed macOS releases, and Linux amd64 Debian packages with in-app updates.\r\n\r\n## Highlights\r\n\r\n- **Six more history sources.** Pi, Oh My Pi (OMP), DeepSeek Harness, GitHub Copilot / VS Code Chat, Hermes Agent, and ZCode join Claude Code, Codex, and Kimi Code.\r\n\r\n## Updates\r\n\r\n### More history sources\r\n\r\n- **Pi:** index messages, images, tools, summaries, and model usage across supported session formats. Reconstruct the selected branch and compacted context, with superseded history available to retrieval where the source can identify it.\r\n\r\n### More faithful existing histories\r\n\r\n- Discover **Codex archived sessions** alongside active sessions, including archived child-thread lookup.\r\n\r\n### Faster live indexing and large archives\r\n\r\n- Replace per-file watching with native/adaptive watchers, addressing macOS file-descriptor exhaustion in large history trees. Watch provider-declared database files directly as well as transcript directories.\r\n\r\n### Retrieval, memories, and DeepSeek Harness\r\n\r\n- Identify the session invoking retrieval with `is_invoking` and `overview().current.session_id`, so agents can distinguish their current context from historical evidence. Query-script content can resolve invocations whose expanded temporary-file path is absent from the transcript; unresolved or ambiguous identity remains unknown.\r\n\r\n### Desktop reading and window behavior\r\n\r\n- Render string-valued tool inputs, including Codex `apply_patch`, as readable multiline blocks, preserving tabs, blank lines, and literal prefixes.\r\n\r\n### Desktop releases and in-app updates\r\n\r\n- Provide native **arm64 and x64 DMG/ZIP packages**, with Developer ID signing, Hardened Runtime, Apple notarization, and stapled tickets. Release automation verifies the extracted packages and their native resources before assembling a draft.\r\n\r\n### Contribution and delivery improvements\r\n\r\n- Add an agent guidance entry point and strengthen contributor guidance for provider adapters, error messages, measured performance, recovery behavior, shared callers, review scope, and commit rationale.\r\n\r\n## Installation\r\n\r\n**macOS:** choose the DMG matching your Mac — **arm64** for Apple Silicon or **x64** for Intel — and install Obelisk.app in Applications.\r\n\r\n**Linux Intel/AMD 64-bit:** download `Obelisk-0.2.4-linux-amd64.deb` and install it with:\r\n\r\n```sh\r\nsudo apt install ./Obelisk-0.2.4-linux-amd64.deb\r\n```\r\n\r\nAn existing **0.2.2 installation needs one manual upgrade** to this updater-enabled version. Existing Linux builds without the updater also need that manual upgrade. Later published desktop releases can be installed through the app; Linux installation asks for administrator authorization.\r\n\r\nThis release supplies **macOS DMG/ZIP and Linux amd64 Debian packages**. The Linux installer is verified on Ubuntu 22.04 and 24.04. Linux arm64, AppImage and Windows distribution/update channels remain outside this release. Shared Core, CLI, and DSH work is listed below; the CLI keeps its separate npm release lifecycle (currently 0.2.6).\r\n\r\n## Included work\r\n\r\nAll 86 merged pull requests since [v0.2.2](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/releases/tag/v0.2.2), including shared Core, CLI, DSH plugin and development work.\r\n\r\n- **@tommy0103 (owner)** — Provider, query and persistence improvements; native/adaptive watching and bounded incremental indexing; DeepSeek Harness and DSH plugin/context-window integration; contributor guidance; signed macOS releases and Sparkle updates; Linux Debian releases and in-app upgrades; reader-position fixes; and release preparation. Included PRs: [#31](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/31), [#36](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/36), [#44](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/44), [#50](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/50), [#51](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/51), [#52](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/52), [#53](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/53), [#54](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/54), [#57](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/57), [#58](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/58), [#68](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/68), [#83](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/83), [#88](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/88), [#90](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/90), [#95](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/95), [#100](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/100), [#102](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/102), [#103](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/103), [#106](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/106), [#110](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/110), [#111](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/111), [#113](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/113), [#120](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/120), [#121](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/121), [#123](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/123), [#126](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/126), [#129](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/129), [#131](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/131), [#133](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/133), [#136](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/136), [#141](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/141), [#149](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/149), [#154](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/154), [#182](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/182), [#188](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/188), [#192](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/192), [#200](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/200), [#207](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/207), [#208](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/208), [#210](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/210), [#211](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/211), [#212](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/212), [#213](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/213), [#214](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/pull/214).\r\n\r\n**Full changes:** [v0.2.2 → v0.2.4 release source](https://github.com/tommy0103/obelisk/compare/v0.2.2...34ee239537e9af22ede2e49209fd7e89c8c47738).\r\n"}]
+"""#
+
+@Suite struct ObeliskChangelogRecipeTests {
+
+    /// Detection and install come from the bundle's own Sparkle feed. The recipe
+    /// is there for the notes: the appcast's `<description>` is Markdown without
+    /// `sparkle:format`, so the pane would show it raw, and the feed holds one
+    /// release only.
+    @Test func readsGitHubReleases() throws {
+        let recipe = try #require(
+            ChangelogRecipeRegistry.recipe(forBundleID: "com.obelisk.app"))
+        #expect(recipe.structuredFormat == .gitHubReleases)
+        #expect(recipe.mode == .json)
+        #expect(recipe.source.host == "api.github.com")
+        #expect(recipe.source.path == "/repos/tommy0103/obelisk/releases")
+    }
+
+    /// Newest first, `v` stripped so the rail version matches
+    /// `CFBundleShortVersionString` (`v0.2.5` → `0.2.5`), dates as ISO days.
+    @Test func versionsAndDatesMatchTheInstalledVersionScheme() throws {
+        let log = try #require(
+            StructuredChangelogDecoder.decodeGitHubReleases(obeliskReleasesFixture, maxEntries: 20))
+        #expect(log.entries.map(\.version) == ["0.2.5", "0.2.4"])
+        #expect(log.entries.map(\.date) == ["2026-10-08", "2026-10-02"])
+    }
+
+    /// The `##` / `###` headings survive as heading blocks (the pane draws them as
+    /// headings), and none of them is also a change line.
+    @Test func keepsTheVendorsHeadingsAsHeadings() throws {
+        let log = try #require(
+            StructuredChangelogDecoder.decodeGitHubReleases(obeliskReleasesFixture, maxEntries: 20))
+        let latest = try #require(log.entries.first)
+        let headings = latest.content.compactMap { block -> String? in
+            if case .heading(let text) = block { return text }
+            return nil
+        }
+        #expect(headings.contains("Highlights"))
+        #expect(headings.contains("Complete session browsing"))
+        #expect(headings.contains("Kiro CLI history"))
+        #expect(headings.contains("Included work"))
+        #expect(!latest.items.contains { headings.contains($0) })
+    }
+
+    /// The CRLF body must come out the same way: a stray `\r` on a heading line
+    /// would stop it matching as a heading.
+    @Test func aCRLFBodyKeepsItsHeadingsToo() throws {
+        let log = try #require(
+            StructuredChangelogDecoder.decodeGitHubReleases(obeliskReleasesFixture, maxEntries: 20))
+        let previous = try #require(log.entries.last)
+        let headings = previous.content.compactMap { block -> String? in
+            if case .heading(let text) = block { return text }
+            return nil
+        }
+        #expect(headings.contains("Highlights"))
+        #expect(headings.contains("More history sources"))
+        #expect(!headings.contains { $0.contains("\r") })
+    }
+}
